        },
        "semanticLevel": "detail",
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='width:100%;min-height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;container-type:inline-size;background:#ffffff;font-family:Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 clamp(20px,5cqw,40px) 64px'><div style='padding-top:32px'><header data-role='masthead'><div style='padding:10px 0;border-top:3px solid #0f172a;border-bottom:1px solid #0f172a;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>PRIMITIVE · declaration</div><h1 style='margin:40px 0 0;font:500 clamp(40px,9.5cqw,72px)/.98 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.025em;text-wrap:balance;color:#0f172a'>Declaration</h1><p style='margin:18px 0 0;max-width:36ch;font:italic 400 clamp(20px,3.6cqw,26px)/1.35 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#475569'>The contract that gives a graph its identity and says what it exposes.</p></header></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-start;gap:32px 40px'><div style='flex:999 1 380px;min-width:0'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Meaning</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What is this thing?</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Declaration is the artifact-level contract that gives a graph durable identity, intent, authority, dependencies, and exposed surfaces. It is a graph&#39;s semantic anchor and its implicit root — the entry point every consumer reaches first, without inventing a separate root node merely for plumbing.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A graph without a Declaration is a fragment: content that hasn&#39;t yet been given an identity of its own.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A graph is a scene by default. Its nodes are placed by hand and its edges are drawn, and that arrangement is what a reader sees. When the Declaration lands on a pinned node, that node is the ground beneath it: a picture, a poster or a page.</p></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0'><section data-role='settings' style='padding-top:10px;border-top:3px solid #0f172a'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0f172a'>At a glance</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;column-gap:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #cbd5e1'><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Node type</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>declaration</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Shown when browsing</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>No, only when editing</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>One per graph</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>it is the graph’s root</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Exposes</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>Surfaces, a landing surface, ports</dd></dl></section></div></div></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Invariants</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What makes this thing a Declaration, specifically?</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Exactly one authoritative Declaration per graph (a fragment may have none; it never has two).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Owns <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.identity</code> — <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graphId</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>name</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>version</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>description</code> — for the graph as a whole.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Owns <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.intent</code> — <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>kind</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>scope</code> — stating what kind of artifact this graph is.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Is reachable as the graph&#39;s implicit root port even when no other node explicitly claims that role.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Every other node that belongs to this graph shares its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graphId</code>.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A View representation is required before each terminal Glyph. Rendering glyph-shaped artwork does not require an icon View classification.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Contract</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What data belongs to it?</strong></p><pre style='margin:0 0 16px;padding:14px 16px;overflow-x:auto;white-space:pre;background:#f1f5f9;border-left:3px solid #6d28d9;font:400 13px/1.55 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#0f172a'>identity      { graphId, name, version, description }\nintent        { kind, scope }\ndeclaration   { kind, defaultSurfaceId, surfaces[] }\nauthority     { mutation{allowCreate,allowUpdate,allowDelete}, actors{humans,agents,tools} }\nsettings      { layout, autoSave }\ndocument      { url }</pre><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Required at minimum: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>identity.graphId</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>identity.name</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>identity.version</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>identity.description</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>intent.kind</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>intent.scope</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>declaration.kind</code>.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Removed, 2026-09-23: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>declaration.targetMode</code>.</strong> It declared whether this graph, when bridged to, presents itself as a single artifact (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>&#39;artifact&#39;</code>) or a browsable bundle (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>&#39;library&#39;</code>) — traced end to end through <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>deriveBridgeTargetResolutionFromGraph()</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>creationVocabulary.js</code>, it never actually changed that outcome: the branch that matters checks a literal <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exportKind === &#39;class&#39;</code>, and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exportKind</code> is decided first by an explicit request, then by a per-surface declared <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exportKind</code>, and only as a last resort by whether the graph already self-identifies as a node-class another way. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>targetMode</code> never won a tie in the paths that were checked. What actually makes a graph a &quot;kit&quot; is a fact already present and checked elsewhere: whether it has any <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>role:&#39;export&#39;</code> Bridge nodes at all (see the Bridge primitive&#39;s Behavior section). Removed the field, its editor dropdown, and its validation; the live derivation now reads <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>intent.kind</code> where it used to read the redundant flag.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Also removed, 2026-09-23: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>settings.github</code> on the Declaration node itself.</strong> It looked like part of the node&#39;s own data — the Contract table above used to list it right next to <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>layout</code>/<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>autoSave</code> — but the save path never actually puts it there. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>Toolbar.js</code>&#39;s save snapshot computes GitHub settings and hands them to <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>setManifestSettings()</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>manifestUtils.js</code>, and that function destructures <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>github</code> straight back out (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>const { github: _ignoredGithub, ...authoritySettings } = settings</code>) and deletes it again if it somehow survives, every single save. GitHub repo/path/branch/installationId live only in the *file&#39;s* own top-level <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>settings.github</code>, one level up from any node&#39;s <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data</code> — a different object entirely, needed by the commit flow specifically because it carries <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>branch</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>installationId</code>, which a bare ref can&#39;t encode. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>document.url</code> (kept — still in the contract table above) already carries everything else a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>github://</code> ref would: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>repo</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>path</code> are both re-derivable from it via <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>parseGithubRef()</code>. Two defensive read paths (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>interGraphEdge.js</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>reciprocalExpansionBridge.js</code>) still check <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.settings.github.path</code> as a fallback; that&#39;s checking for a value current code guarantees is never there.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Ports and Relationships</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What semantic relationships does it expose?</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A graph offers its presentation through <strong>Surfaces</strong> (see the Surface primitive). The Declaration has <strong>one port for them, and that port accepts any number of Surfaces.</strong> Each Surface is a chain of Views that ends in a Glyph.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Surfaces:</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>surface</code> (repeatable) — one edge per Surface, each pointing at the <strong>first View</strong> of that Surface&#39;s chain. The edge carries the Surface&#39;s id (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.surfaceId</code>), which is its only identity: rename it there, delete the edge to remove the Surface, and if two edges share an id the first wins. The graph canvas is a Surface; a web view, a 3D view or a map view is another. They are all the same kind of thing.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The chain.</strong> View → View → … → <strong>Glyph</strong>. Views run richest to most compact, and each can claim its own continuous resolution threshold (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>authoredViewResolution()</code>, already real and unbounded). A host walks the chain and takes the richest View that fits.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Every Surface begins with a View.</strong> There is no glyph-only Surface. A graph that should show as just a glyph uses a View representation that renders glyph-shaped artwork (no icon category is required), and ends its chain with its ordinary Glyph.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The Glyph ends the chain.</strong> It is the Surface&#39;s compact symbolic identity and the floor of its ladder, so <strong>every Surface carries its own glyph</strong> instead of the whole graph sharing one. A web view and a 3D view can each have a mark that suits them.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>The rungs formerly named <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>default-view</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>summary-view</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>icon-view</code> are now just positions in a chain, not separate relationships. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>declaration.defaultSurfaceId</code> says which Surface a host uses when it doesn&#39;t ask for one.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>This <strong>supersedes</strong> five relationships: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>default-view</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>summary-view</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>icon-view</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>view-sequence</code> and the standalone <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>glyph</code>. They are not removed. A graph written with them still loads, and is read as <strong>one</strong> default Surface, never one Surface per relationship; the surface migrator (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>npm run migrate:surfaces</code>) rewrites such a graph to the Surface chain. Their ports stay on the Declaration so old edges still land, marked legacy.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A Surface chooses what renders <strong>inside</strong> the graph&#39;s frame. It never resizes the frame, reflows neighbors or moves the camera.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Status.</strong> In the app now: the <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>surface</code> port; new graphs are created with one Surface; the editor lets you drag from the <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>surface</code> handle to add a Surface and from a View&#39;s next-view handle to extend its chain, and lists each Surface with its chain and Glyph in the Declaration editor; the resolver reads Surfaces and reads legacy graphs as one default Surface; per-Surface glyph and resolution ladder; a host may say what it can render and is never handed a Surface it cannot; the validator checks a well-formed chain per Surface; and the documents an LLM or an agent reads describe Surfaces. <strong>Not yet:</strong> the edge classes (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>surface</code>; <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>view-sequence</code> ending at a glyph) are changed locally but not published; the workspace and camera do not yet ask for a Surface other than the default; nothing renders a 3D View; the Declaration node&#39;s own card is still drawn by dedicated code, because the Declaration template is still a draft (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>templates/declaration-template</code>).</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Malformed chains.</strong> Imported files do not pass through the editor&#39;s edge-class rules, so the resolver decides on its own, the same way every time, and every case still shows something: a cycle stops at the first repeated node; a branch follows the first outgoing edge; a Glyph ends the walk and anything after it is ignored; a chain that reaches something other than a View or Glyph stops there; a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>surface</code> edge whose head is not a View is skipped; a chain with no Glyph falls back to the Declaration&#39;s own <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.glyph</code>; a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>defaultSurfaceId</code> that names no Surface falls to the first. Each is reported as a warning, never an error. Still to check live: two Surfaces sharing one View.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>The Declaration&#39;s own Views.</strong> No relationship for them is needed. A Port, and the Declaration as the implicit root Port, connects to Views through the relationships it already has. The Declaration *node&#39;s* own presentation comes from a Declaration template graph (see Representations).</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Embedding behavior:</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>default-handle</code> — points at a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graphReference</code> node. When this graph appears as a node inside a *parent* graph, this is what a new edge dragged from its collapsed handle actually targets — a fact about this graph&#39;s own embedding, not about how it presents itself.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Boundary:</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>boundary-begin</code> / <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>boundary-end</code> — the ends of a hand-authored chain of <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>membrane</code>-type control-point nodes, linked by <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>next</code> edges (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>membraneChain.js</code>), forming a custom membrane *shape*. An alternative to the auto-computed rectangular extent (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graphExtent()</code>/<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>defaultMembraneBounds()</code>) for a graph that needs a boundary the rectangle can&#39;t express.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Entry points:</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>port</code> (repeatable) — an exposed, named entry point.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>landing-surface</code> — the single node a portal lands on when entering this graph, usually a Content node. At most one. When it is pinned it is the graph’s ground: drawn beneath its edges and its other nodes. Despite the name, it is not a Surface in the sense above.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Each relationship <strong>assigns</strong> another node to a named role. The role lives on the Declaration&#39;s edge to that node, not as a property intrinsic to the node itself — the same node could be assigned a different role, or none, without changing anything about the node.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Behavior</h2><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Agreed semantics</h3><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The Declaration supplies artifact identity, exposure and authority. It has no independent program that runs merely because the node exists. Consumers use its contract for navigation, validation, presentation and save ownership. The graph-facing Surface chains and the Declaration node&#39;s own class presentation have separate owners.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The relationship grammar, defaults and validator read Surface chains and preserve legacy relationships. Navigation uses exposed Ports and the landing surface. The Declaration node still has a dedicated ManifestNode rendering fallback; the template/class path is not universally substituted. A pinned landing surface is drawn as the ground: beneath the graph’s edges and other nodes, and not lifted over them when selected. A document is the special case: a graph that hides its pieces and shows a page assembled from them as its ground.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Known gaps</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The Declaration template and uniform node presentation, Surface authoring polish, host/3D selection and live smoke evidence remain tracked at tlz://twilite.zone/tasks/declaration-surfaces/root.node. A specification update does not complete those runtime tasks.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Representations</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>How is it represented?</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Like any other node.</strong> A Declaration takes part in view resolution exactly as a class node or a template node does, with no privileged path of its own: a host resolves the richest View that fits the room and context it has — the same resolver, the same rule, for every node. Its Surfaces (see Ports and Relationships) describe the graph; its own card is resolved the same way, from its own default View.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Its default is a View, not a special case.</strong> The compact identity card (name, description, version) is simply the default View a Declaration comes with, the way any class or template comes with a default. When the author wires Views to it, those take over and resolve like any others. Nothing needs to be switched off to do this.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Collapsed, it shows a Glyph</strong>, the same as every other node, not a separate &quot;declaration mark&quot;.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Two presentations, two owners.</strong> The Surfaces on the Declaration describe how the <strong>graph</strong> presents itself, embedded or opened. How the Declaration <strong>node</strong> presents itself on a canvas is a separate question, answered by its own default View through the same resolver. A Declaration is never rendered by the chains it exposes, so nothing can recurse into itself.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Custom Declaration rendering was tried early on and set aside as too troublesome. That was when a Declaration needed a renderer of its own. It doesn&#39;t now: presentation is Surfaces, Views and Glyphs, independent of any one renderer, and the Declaration goes through them like everything else.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>The card comes from a template.</strong> A Declaration template graph will hold all of the Declaration&#39;s parts, and the Declaration resolves through that graph by the ordinary class binding, exactly as any class or template node does. There is no special relationship for it: a Port connects to Views, and the Declaration is the implicit root Port. An author who wants a different card uses a different template. Until that template is final the card is the built-in default View, drawn by dedicated code (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ManifestNode</code>) rather than through the resolver.</p><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Implementation boundary</h3><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The preceding uniform-template presentation is the agreed direction. It is not a claim that every Declaration currently takes that path; see Behavior for the dedicated renderer and remaining task.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Surface selection and resolution do not implicitly resize the workspace frame or reflow neighboring graphs. Focusing a graph draws it at its own authored size, whatever size it was dragged to in the workspace while it was not focused. Manual resizing is explicit: on a focused graph it sets the graph’s own window, on an unfocused one only its size in this workspace. Zoom scales the workspace rather than changing authored dimensions. Internal nodes, edges, handles and pointer targets are clipped to the frame; exposed boundary controls stay accessible. A View representation keeps the workspace title bar; the terminal Glyph does not acquire one.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive</h2><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Declaration does not render content. Rendering the graph&#39;s actual material is View and Content&#39;s job; Declaration only names which node fills each presentation role.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Declaration does not own node positions or layout. Where nodes sit on the canvas is unrelated to identity.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Declaration does not define *how* an exposed view looks — only *which* node is assigned to that role.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Declaration does not grant runtime capability beyond what <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>authority</code> explicitly states. Absence of a grant means the action is not permitted, not that it&#39;s merely undocumented.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Declaration is not a template. It describes one graph&#39;s own identity, not a reusable composition pattern for building other graphs (see Templates).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Declaration does not vouch for which custom node types are safe to use in the graph. That authority comes from Bridges, one per type, each pointing at a real class definition.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Examples</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Examples live outside the primitive, referenced, not embedded.</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Weekend Reading Group</strong> is a small plan graph whose Declaration is a Custom Declaration: bound to the Custom Declaration class, allowed to use it by an import bridge, drawn as a card that shows its own name, kind and version, and edited with the complete form. It has one Surface (a plan, a summary, an icon, then a glyph) and a landing page, and it is laid out the way the Declaration Template teaches. Open it with the portal beside this node.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The Declaration Template itself is the other reference: a page that explains each part of a Declaration and walks through building one.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>No canon graph has a Custom Declaration yet; the example linked here is still in a personal library.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Where this lives</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Source (twilite repository):</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/declarationRelationshipHandles.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/systemNodeDefaults.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/graphNavigationAddress.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/validators/validateGraphInvariants.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/Nodes/ManifestNode.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/declarationTemplate.test.js</code></li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Focused tests:</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/surfaceChains.test.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/declarationDefaults.test.js</code></li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Structural tests establish shape and links; they do not establish browser behavior.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:40px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:stretch;gap:28px 32px'><div style='flex:1 1 190px;min-width:0'><section data-role='route' data-prominence='primary' style='height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding-top:12px;border-top:3px solid #6d28d9'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:500 26px/1.1 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#0f172a'>Weekend Reading Group</h2><p style='margin:0 0 10px;font:400 14px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>A worked example: templates/declaration-template/example.</p><a data-role='route-link' href='github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/declaration-template/example/root.node' style='margin-top:auto;font:700 13px/20px system-ui;text-decoration:none;color:#6d28d9'>Open the example →</a></section></div></div></div></div></div>"
