    {
      "id": "decl-meaning",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": -640
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Meaning",
        "body": "**What is this thing?**\n\nDeclaration is the artifact-level contract that gives a graph durable identity, intent, authority, dependencies, and exposed surfaces. It is a graph's semantic anchor and its implicit root — the entry point every consumer reaches first, without inventing a separate root node merely for plumbing.\n\nA graph without a Declaration is a fragment: content that hasn't yet been given an identity of its own.\n\nA graph is a scene by default. Its nodes are placed by hand and its edges are drawn, and that arrangement is what a reader sees. When the Declaration lands on a pinned node, that node is the ground beneath it: a picture, a poster or a page.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "definitionKey": "page-section",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "page-section",
          "sourceRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node",
