    {
      "id": "decl-contract",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Contract",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": -280
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Contract",
        "body": "**What data belongs to it?**\n\n```text\nidentity      { graphId, name, version, description }\nintent        { kind, scope }\ndeclaration   { kind, defaultSurfaceId, surfaces[] }\nauthority     { mutation{allowCreate,allowUpdate,allowDelete}, actors{humans,agents,tools} }\nsettings      { layout, autoSave }\ndocument      { url }\n```\n\nRequired at minimum: `identity.graphId`, `identity.name`, `identity.version`, `identity.description`, `intent.kind`, `intent.scope`, `declaration.kind`.\n\n**Removed, 2026-09-23: `declaration.targetMode`.** It declared whether this graph, when bridged to, presents itself as a single artifact (`'artifact'`) or a browsable bundle (`'library'`) — traced end to end through `deriveBridgeTargetResolutionFromGraph()` in `creationVocabulary.js`, it never actually changed that outcome: the branch that matters checks a literal `exportKind === 'class'`, and `exportKind` is decided first by an explicit request, then by a per-surface declared `exportKind`, and only as a last resort by whether the graph already self-identifies as a node-class another way. `targetMode` never won a tie in the paths that were checked. What actually makes a graph a \"kit\" is a fact already present and checked elsewhere: whether it has any `role:'export'` Bridge nodes at all (see the Bridge primitive's Behavior section). Removed the field, its editor dropdown, and its validation; the live derivation now reads `intent.kind` where it used to read the redundant flag.\n\n**Also removed, 2026-09-23: `settings.github` on the Declaration node itself.** It looked like part of the node's own data — the Contract table above used to list it right next to `layout`/`autoSave` — but the save path never actually puts it there. `Toolbar.js`'s save snapshot computes GitHub settings and hands them to `setManifestSettings()` in `manifestUtils.js`, and that function destructures `github` straight back out (`const { github: _ignoredGithub, ...authoritySettings } = settings`) and deletes it again if it somehow survives, every single save. GitHub repo/path/branch/installationId live only in the *file's* own top-level `settings.github`, one level up from any node's `data` — a different object entirely, needed by the commit flow specifically because it carries `branch` and `installationId`, which a bare ref can't encode. `document.url` (kept — still in the contract table above) already carries everything else a `github://` ref would: `repo` and `path` are both re-derivable from it via `parseGithubRef()`. Two defensive read paths (`interGraphEdge.js`, `reciprocalExpansionBridge.js`) still check `data.settings.github.path` as a fallback; that's checking for a value current code guarantees is never there.",
