    {
      "id": "decl-ports",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Ports and Relationships",
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": -280
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Ports and Relationships",
        "body": "**What semantic relationships does it expose?**\n\nA graph offers its presentation through **Surfaces** (see the Surface primitive). The Declaration has **one port for them, and that port accepts any number of Surfaces.** Each Surface is a chain of Views that ends in a Glyph.\n\n**Surfaces:**\n\n- `surface` (repeatable) — one edge per Surface, each pointing at the **first View** of that Surface's chain. The edge carries the Surface's id (`data.surfaceId`), which is its only identity: rename it there, delete the edge to remove the Surface, and if two edges share an id the first wins. The graph canvas is a Surface; a web view, a 3D view or a map view is another. They are all the same kind of thing.\n- **The chain.** View → View → … → **Glyph**. Views run richest to most compact, and each can claim its own continuous resolution threshold (`authoredViewResolution()`, already real and unbounded). A host walks the chain and takes the richest View that fits.\n- **Every Surface begins with a View.** There is no glyph-only Surface. A graph that should show as just a glyph uses a View representation that renders glyph-shaped artwork (no icon category is required), and ends its chain with its ordinary Glyph.\n- **The Glyph ends the chain.** It is the Surface's compact symbolic identity and the floor of its ladder, so **every Surface carries its own glyph** instead of the whole graph sharing one. A web view and a 3D view can each have a mark that suits them.\n- The rungs formerly named `default-view`, `summary-view` and `icon-view` are now just positions in a chain, not separate relationships. `declaration.defaultSurfaceId` says which Surface a host uses when it doesn't ask for one.\n\nThis **supersedes** five relationships: `default-view`, `summary-view`, `icon-view`, `view-sequence` and the standalone `glyph`. They are not removed. A graph written with them still loads, and is read as **one** default Surface, never one Surface per relationship; the surface migrator (`npm run migrate:surfaces`) rewrites such a graph to the Surface chain. Their ports stay on the Declaration so old edges still land, marked legacy.\n\nA Surface chooses what renders **inside** the graph's frame. It never resizes the frame, reflows neighbors or moves the camera.\n\n**Status.** In the app now: the `surface` port; new graphs are created with one Surface; the editor lets you drag from the `surface` handle to add a Surface and from a View's next-view handle to extend its chain, and lists each Surface with its chain and Glyph in the Declaration editor; the resolver reads Surfaces and reads legacy graphs as one default Surface; per-Surface glyph and resolution ladder; a host may say what it can render and is never handed a Surface it cannot; the validator checks a well-formed chain per Surface; and the documents an LLM or an agent reads describe Surfaces. **Not yet:** the edge classes (`surface`; `view-sequence` ending at a glyph) are changed locally but not published; the workspace and camera do not yet ask for a Surface other than the default; nothing renders a 3D View; the Declaration node's own card is still drawn by dedicated code, because the Declaration template is still a draft (`templates/declaration-template`).\n\n**Malformed chains.** Imported files do not pass through the editor's edge-class rules, so the resolver decides on its own, the same way every time, and every case still shows something: a cycle stops at the first repeated node; a branch follows the first outgoing edge; a Glyph ends the walk and anything after it is ignored; a chain that reaches something other than a View or Glyph stops there; a `surface` edge whose head is not a View is skipped; a chain with no Glyph falls back to the Declaration's own `data.glyph`; a `defaultSurfaceId` that names no Surface falls to the first. Each is reported as a warning, never an error. Still to check live: two Surfaces sharing one View.\n\n**The Declaration's own Views.** No relationship for them is needed. A Port, and the Declaration as the implicit root Port, connects to Views through the relationships it already has. The Declaration *node's* own presentation comes from a Declaration template graph (see Representations).\n\n**Embedding behavior:**\n\n- `default-handle` — points at a `graphReference` node. When this graph appears as a node inside a *parent* graph, this is what a new edge dragged from its collapsed handle actually targets — a fact about this graph's own embedding, not about how it presents itself.\n\n**Boundary:**\n\n- `boundary-begin` / `boundary-end` — the ends of a hand-authored chain of `membrane`-type control-point nodes, linked by `next` edges (`membraneChain.js`), forming a custom membrane *shape*. An alternative to the auto-computed rectangular extent (`graphExtent()`/`defaultMembraneBounds()`) for a graph that needs a boundary the rectangle can't express.\n\n**Entry points:**\n\n- `port` (repeatable) — an exposed, named entry point.\n- `landing-surface` — the single node a portal lands on when entering this graph, usually a Content node. At most one. When it is pinned it is the graph’s ground: drawn beneath its edges and its other nodes. Despite the name, it is not a Surface in the sense above.\n\nEach relationship **assigns** another node to a named role. The role lives on the Declaration's edge to that node, not as a property intrinsic to the node itself — the same node could be assigned a different role, or none, without changing anything about the node.",
