    {
      "id": "decl-behavior",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Behavior",
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": 440
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Behavior",
        "body": "## Agreed semantics\n\nThe Declaration supplies artifact identity, exposure and authority. It has no independent program that runs merely because the node exists. Consumers use its contract for navigation, validation, presentation and save ownership. The graph-facing Surface chains and the Declaration node's own class presentation have separate owners.\n\nThe relationship grammar, defaults and validator read Surface chains and preserve legacy relationships. Navigation uses exposed Ports and the landing surface. The Declaration node still has a dedicated ManifestNode rendering fallback; the template/class path is not universally substituted. A pinned landing surface is drawn as the ground: beneath the graph’s edges and other nodes, and not lifted over them when selected. A document is the special case: a graph that hides its pieces and shows a page assembled from them as its ground.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "definitionKey": "page-section",
