    {
      "id": "decl-representations",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Representations",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": 80
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Representations",
        "body": "**How is it represented?**\n\n**Like any other node.** A Declaration takes part in view resolution exactly as a class node or a template node does, with no privileged path of its own: a host resolves the richest View that fits the room and context it has — the same resolver, the same rule, for every node. Its Surfaces (see Ports and Relationships) describe the graph; its own card is resolved the same way, from its own default View.\n\n- **Its default is a View, not a special case.** The compact identity card (name, description, version) is simply the default View a Declaration comes with, the way any class or template comes with a default. When the author wires Views to it, those take over and resolve like any others. Nothing needs to be switched off to do this.\n- **Collapsed, it shows a Glyph**, the same as every other node, not a separate \"declaration mark\".\n- **Two presentations, two owners.** The Surfaces on the Declaration describe how the **graph** presents itself, embedded or opened. How the Declaration **node** presents itself on a canvas is a separate question, answered by its own default View through the same resolver. A Declaration is never rendered by the chains it exposes, so nothing can recurse into itself.\n\nCustom Declaration rendering was tried early on and set aside as too troublesome. That was when a Declaration needed a renderer of its own. It doesn't now: presentation is Surfaces, Views and Glyphs, independent of any one renderer, and the Declaration goes through them like everything else.\n\n**The card comes from a template.** A Declaration template graph will hold all of the Declaration's parts, and the Declaration resolves through that graph by the ordinary class binding, exactly as any class or template node does. There is no special relationship for it: a Port connects to Views, and the Declaration is the implicit root Port. An author who wants a different card uses a different template. Until that template is final the card is the built-in default View, drawn by dedicated code (`ManifestNode`) rather than through the resolver.\n\n## Implementation boundary\n\nThe preceding uniform-template presentation is the agreed direction. It is not a claim that every Declaration currently takes that path; see Behavior for the dedicated renderer and remaining task.\n\nSurface selection and resolution do not implicitly resize the workspace frame or reflow neighboring graphs. Focusing a graph draws it at its own authored size, whatever size it was dragged to in the workspace while it was not focused. Manual resizing is explicit: on a focused graph it sets the graph’s own window, on an unfocused one only its size in this workspace. Zoom scales the workspace rather than changing authored dimensions. Internal nodes, edges, handles and pointer targets are clipped to the frame; exposed boundary controls stay accessible. A View representation keeps the workspace title bar; the terminal Glyph does not acquire one.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
